/* ============================================================
   UT TURF FARM — BAKED FARM GEOMETRY & REFERENCE DATA
   ------------------------------------------------------------
   Everything here describes the ground itself: the outline of every plot,
   the alley network, the ten alley work zones, each plot's turf information,
   which plots are split, and the mowing/irrigation assignments.

   It lived inside UT-TurfFarm-App.html as six single lines totalling ~210 KB
   (one of them 86,000 characters wide), which made the app file slow to edit
   and made two concurrent edits collide on ground nobody was actually
   changing. It is the same data, formatted so a diff is readable.

   This file must sit beside UT-TurfFarm-App.html and is loaded by a plain
   <script src="farm-geo.js"> tag ahead of the app's own code, so every global
   below is defined before anything reads it. No build step, no module system —
   the PWA can cache it as a separate asset.

   These six globals are exactly what moves into Postgres in Phase 2
   (see Editable-Map-Backend-Plan.md). When that lands, this file is replaced
   by a fetch, and nothing else in the app has to change: the app only ever
   reads the names, never the file.

   NOT hand-edited:
     ALLEY_ZONES  — generated by tools/make-alley-zones.py
     PLOTS_DATA   — shape edits are layered over this at runtime by PE_STORE;
                    to bake an edit in, use Map -> Export and paste the result
                    here, then Reset all in the app.

   A standing gotcha, kept here because the schema will need to encode it:
   the CAFS polygons are a SYNTHETIC GRID drawn to sit in roughly the right
   place. Their geometry is not survey-accurate and their computed area is
   meaningless. Plot area for spray rates comes from PLOT_INFO's
   "Area (sq ft)" field and from nowhere else — never from the polygon.
   ============================================================ */

